<div id="outline-container-continuing-my-experiment-with-semi-retirement-my-own-interests" class="outline-3">
<h3 id="continuing-my-experiment-with-semi-retirement-my-own-interests"><a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/feed/index.xml#continuing-my-experiment-with-semi-retirement-my-own-interests">My own interests</a></h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-continuing-my-experiment-with-semi-retirement-my-own-interests">
<p>
I'd love to keep tinkering around with Emacs, perhaps livestreaming so that people can ask questions along the way.
I'd like to write about and package up my experiments so that other people can build on the ideas.
I've been having lots of fun challenging my brain with French, too.
In addition to the satisfaction of feeling more things click in my brain and the delight of helping the kiddo explore it outside school, I get even more reasons to play with my Emacs setup and my workflows. (Not that I needed the excuse, but still.)
</p>

<p>
So: a slow life, a "let's try things and see" sort of life, where we can experiment with things that might not yield immediate results, where it might not even be so much about getting answers but more about learning how to ask questions and listen.
  What could this look like?
    I think I might try a year-long experiment with proper retirement, zero consulting: spending time on the things that matter to me while funding expenses from savings and investments.
</p>

<p>
Time to see if my <a href="https://ledger-cli.org/">Ledger</a> record-keeping will pay off when it comes to helping me calculate adjusted cost basis and capital gains. Let's see if I can file our taxes properly.
    There's paperwork, but it's probably manageable. (I still feel a sense of satisfaction from the time the Canada Revenue Agency asked about the medical expenses we claimed one year and I sent back a PDF with a proper table of contents that linked to each receipt, yay LaTeX.)
</p>

<p>
The intrinsic fun of learning things and trying things out should be plenty to keep me busy, and there's always helping out with the Emacs community or volunteering with Bike Brigade.
    We don't need to figure out grand, universal truths. It's enough to keep figuring out what might work in this particular moment for our peculiar selves, and perhaps share those notes in case they happen to resonate with someone.
</p>

<p>
Do I want to set up a Github Sponsors page, Patreon community, or Liberapay tip jar, like <a href="https://github.com/freetonik/support-emacs-community-devs">many cool Emacs people on the Internet</a>? Not really, not for now. Times are rough for a lot of people, and I'm in a luckier position than most. I think I have enough for most of the things I want, and I like to consider those things carefully anyway. If people do want to share something, <a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2015/02/5-year-experiment-conversation-anxious-side-sharing-time-might-better-giving-money/">time would be amazing</a>. I'm slowly getting the hang of paying people to help me learn, as in the case of <a href="https://italki.com">French tutors</a> for pronunciation and conversation and with <a href="https://protesilaos.com/coach/">Prot's Emacs coaching</a> as a way of adding another perspective to some of my livestreams. I'm sure there are all sorts of things I could learn from people or work with people on.
</p>

<p>
Let's see what this retirement thing is like!
</p>
